What we changed
===============

Every Python file: the import name pylint_home_assistant became
ha_custom_pylint, so the package can sit beside core's without a clash.
checkers/entity_unique_id_format.py differs in that one import line only,
which the shorter name let ruff format join onto one line.

Change 1: a custom integration is the integration. Core decides which code is
an integration by its module name, under homeassistant.components. These files
also accept custom_components.<domain>[.x], and a bare <domain>[.x] when
<domain> resolves to a custom_components/<domain>/ directory holding a
manifest.json, which is how pylint names the modules when it lints
custom_components/<domain> from the repository root. A repo-root tests/
package is taken to test the one integration in custom_components/.

  helpers/module_info.py                 parse_module, is_integration_module and
                                         get_module_platform accept both names;
                                         parse_import_source parses the module a
                                         called name was imported from.
  helpers/integration.py                 read_manifest finds the domain of a bare
                                         name; custom_test_domain names the
                                         integration a repo-root tests/ tests.
  checkers/imports.py                    C7403 fires on the custom_components.<domain>
                                         spelling of the integration's own path; a
                                         repo-root test module importing its own
                                         integration is treated as core treats
                                         tests.components.<domain> (no C7405 or C7407).
  checkers/actions/swallowed_exceptions.py
                                         a decorator defined in the integration's own
                                         modules is inspected, as core inspects those
                                         under homeassistant.
  checkers/tests/direct_async_setup_entry.py
  checkers/tests/direct_async_migrate_entry.py
  checkers/tests/direct_async_setup.py
  checkers/tests/direct_async_unload_entry.py
                                         a called name is matched on the import that
                                         binds it, so an aliased import no longer
                                         evades W7418, W7420, W7421, W7422 or W7426.

Change 2: R7403 reads a custom repository's conftest files. Core's walk in
checkers/tests/redundant_usefixtures.py stops below the tests root. Ours also
reads tests/conftest.py and the repository-root conftest.py, where a custom
repository keeps its fixtures, and stops there.

Everything else is core's code as it stood at the tag.
